# Gmail OAuth2 — Google Cloud Console → OAuth 2.0 credentials
GMAIL_USER=you@yourdomain.com
GMAIL_CLIENT_ID=your-client-id.apps.googleusercontent.com
GMAIL_CLIENT_SECRET=GOCSPX-your-secret
GMAIL_REFRESH_TOKEN=your-refresh-token

# ─── Production host (Clerk) ───────────────────────────────────────────────
# The host the signed-in app is served from. One Vercel project serves both the
# marketing site and the app, so /dashboard is reachable on every host it
# answers on — but Clerk's session cookie is bound to ONE of them. Reaching the
# app on any other host means a session that cannot be read: Clerk bounces to
# sign-in, sign-in succeeds, the cookie lands on the app host, and the redirect
# back cannot see it. That is the sign-in loop.
#
# Set this in production and middleware.ts redirects every other host here.
# Leave it unset locally: localhost and *.vercel.app are exempt anyway.
NEXT_PUBLIC_APP_HOST=app.mdmmarketing.com.au

# Absolute base for links inside emails (intake submissions, reports, workflow
# notifications). A relative path is a dead link in a mail client.
NEXT_PUBLIC_APP_URL=https://app.mdmmarketing.com.au

# Comma-separated. Promotes these emails to super_admin on first sign-in — on a
# fresh Clerk instance nobody holds that role, so without this the first person
# in cannot administer anything.
SUPER_ADMIN_EMAILS=

# Firebase — Console → Project settings → General (RTDB, not Firestore)
NEXT_PUBLIC_FIREBASE_API_KEY=your-api-key
NEXT_PUBLIC_FIREBASE_AUTH_DOMAIN=your-project.firebaseapp.com
NEXT_PUBLIC_FIREBASE_PROJECT_ID=your-project
NEXT_PUBLIC_FIREBASE_APP_ID=1:your-app-id
NEXT_PUBLIC_FIREBASE_DATABASE_URL=https://your-project-default-rtdb.firebasedatabase.app

# ─── Google Drive folder chooser (the Picker) ──────────────────────────────
# The app uses Google's drive.file scope, so it can only see folders it made
# itself. The one way to hand it a folder that already exists — "MD Media HQ"
# and its "Clients" subfolder — is Google's own Picker, and the Picker needs a
# BROWSER API key of its own (the OAuth client id is not enough).
#
# Google Cloud console, on the same project as the OAuth client above:
#   1. APIs & Services → Library → enable "Google Picker API".
#   2. APIs & Services → Credentials → Create credentials → API key.
#      Restrict it: Application restrictions → Websites → the app's host;
#      API restrictions → Google Picker API only.
#   3. Copy the project NUMBER (not the id) from the console home page — that
#      is the app id, and it is what ties the Picker's "you may use this
#      folder" grant to this app.
# Both are public by design: they identify the app, they authorise nothing.
NEXT_PUBLIC_GOOGLE_PICKER_API_KEY=
NEXT_PUBLIC_GOOGLE_PICKER_APP_ID=
